  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_ae1812a9a53242b78529352063ab69cd",
      "statement": "The voice engine setting (off/browser/whisper) is stored per-device in localStorage and deliberately excluded from server-side user-preference sync, so a PC can use browser recognition while an iPhone on the same account simultaneously uses whisper.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/v0.3.x-many-ai-cli-design.md#L648-L649",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:59cefcb9fad5ec951c1f837f61e92c0c1adab1c5205fc3731e3f7f8f9dddad57:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_95d04be13aa2436e88bb081229d5f85f",
      "statement": "Because iOS Safari's WebKit-forced WebSpeech implementation is not usable quality, the mobile voice path separates capture from inference: an iPhone captures 16kHz PCM via AudioWorklet, WAV-encodes it client-side, and posts it to /api/voice/transcribe, which the Hub relays to a Whisper server running on a PC or remote server, without replacing the desktop browser's own built-in recognition.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/v0.3.x-many-ai-cli-design.md#L649-L649",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7eb70257593da06f682a3ddda54a9d260d4fc514f645237f5ca74b08f8da61a6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ec4e545881f54b5c8e6572207e60d961",
      "statement": "Recording deliberately avoids MediaRecorder in favor of WebAudio/AudioWorklet uniformly across browsers specifically to avoid iOS's AAC-only recording output and the server-side ffmpeg dependency that format would otherwise require; the mobile-relay logic was added as a new voice-whisper.ts module rather than modifying the existing voice.ts, to keep the working browser-recognition path untouched.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/v0.3.x-many-ai-cli-design.md#L649-L649",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7eb70257593da06f682a3ddda54a9d260d4fc514f645237f5ca74b08f8da61a6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_39f0bd98386a48c78e49eb1bc23b2a50",
      "statement": "The Whisper path's automatic-send-on-trailing-phrase trigger defaults to off and activates only on explicit opt-in with an exact-match requirement; two client-side filters run before a recording is sent (a near-silent-volume pre-check via AnalyserNode, and a fixed hallucination-phrase filter that discards a known filler phrase only on a full exact match of the entire result, not a partial match), and every discard is surfaced as a toast explaining why rather than being silent.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/v0.3.x-many-ai-cli-design.md#L652-L657",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ce87bc90027f4e4bbae5e571dab16b899916be7450b43e257ed5c7c7bacfa939:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ca867938fe514be4bca835469d50d160",
      "statement": "A server_url pointed at an OpenAI-compatible external API is allowed but treated as an explicit opt-in requiring a UI disclosure that audio leaves the machine; the default configuration only permits a local (127.0.0.1/localhost) server URL.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/v0.3.x-many-ai-cli-design.md#L657-L657",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7eb70257593da06f682a3ddda54a9d260d4fc514f645237f5ca74b08f8da61a6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_564f91efdd5242a9b56c441462ccce66",
      "statement": "On first use of the whisper engine (or at Hub startup if already configured) the Hub spawns whisper-server as a child process on an auto-selected free port; it is guaranteed killed on Hub exit or many-ai-cli stop via a Windows Job Object with KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE, Setpgid plus explicit kill on Linux/macOS, and tini-as-PID1 (init: true) in Docker, and an orphaned whisper-server left from a prior run is detected and reclaimed on Hub restart.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/v0.3.x-many-ai-cli-design.md#L658-L659",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:776f476a79f6ec5b421ec149e5b6cbddf71b2aebe80dd91e2ec74c9c419b9747:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_90d112404b534a08a73328fbc5efba63",
      "statement": "Managed Whisper install ships for Windows x64 only in its initial version, with macOS/Linux steered to a manually configured external server URL; everything it installs lands under ~/.many-ai-cli/whisper/{bin,models,tmp}/, downloads are preceded by a free-disk-space check and verified against a SHA256 checksum before extraction, a failed/partial download is deleted so a retry starts clean, and uninstall is a plain os.RemoveAll of that directory tree leaving no trace elsewhere such as System32.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/v0.3.x-many-ai-cli-design.md#L662-L665",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:76b0c5f3c1bb355c719333fb96458e82753e5d29173383868f298460d312c0f9:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ed586aadd89a43efb2440f145fff4059",
      "statement": "internal/whisperruntime's package doc states it embeds the Windows VC++ runtime DLLs whisper-server links against via go:embed and lays them down next to the managed binary so install needs no System32 write and no machine-wide runtime dependency, everything living under ~/.many-ai-cli/whisper/ so uninstall's RemoveAll leaves zero trace; because the real signed DLLs are gitignored, files/ ships placeholder READMEs so go:embed always finds at least one file and a from-source build succeeds before the real DLLs are added at release/CI time, until which Ensure copies nothing.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/whisperruntime/embed.go#L1-L10",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:65bc5837938f03f55062ab8eb8dad114f9ac0c6cab9fba572213e2d108772587:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_97a1779bb562448c94c7d548540bf5c9",
      "statement": "fetch_windows_runtime.ps1 deliberately never pulls the four VC++ runtime DLLs from a third-party distribution site: it searches a local Visual Studio Redist folder located via vswhere first, then falls back to Microsoft-signed servicing copies in System32, and validates every selected DLL has a valid Authenticode signature naming Microsoft Corporation as the subject and an x64 (0x8664) PE machine type before accepting it.",
      "evidence": [
